  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nClouds have resulted in a very cool day for most of Nebraska,\nalthough mostly clear skies have allowed portions of northern\nKansas to reach the 80s this afternoon. Tonight, another round\nof warm-air-advection showers/storms is expected. That said, \nCAMs generally show the highest coverage remaining to our east.\nAt any rate, the threat for severe weather is low through\ntonight. \n\nA few showers or weak thunderstorms remain possible during the \nmorning and early afternoon on Monday. This does provide a \nlittle uncertainty on how warm we will actually get, but there \nshould be enough clearing in the afternoon that most areas will\nreach the upper 80s to 90s, aided by fairly strong southwest \nwinds (gusts 30-35 MPH). Southeastern areas could see heat index\nvalues approach/exceed 100 degrees briefly Monday afternoon \nbefore the front moves through. \n\nThis front will provide focus for storm development in the late\nafternoon and evening. Coverage and intensity is still a little\nuncertain, but at least a few strong to severe storms are\npossible in the 5PM to midnight timeframe. Roughly the\nsoutheastern half of the area is most favored for severe\nweather. \n\nStorm intensity will wane Monday night, but at least a few \nshowers and storms will persist into Tuesday. The post-frontal\nairmass will be noticably cooler on Tuesday (highs in the upper\n60s and low 70s, depending on cloud cover). Thunderstorms become\nlikely again Tuesday night, especially in southeastern parts of\nthe area, as southwesterly flow aloft interacts with the stalled\nmidlevel boundary across KS and southeastern Neb. \n\nThis pattern then repeats itself through the rest of the\nworkweek. Overall, temperatures remain on the cooler side, \nalthough temperatures could approach climatological normals\n(upper 70s to low 80s) on Friday. Daily chances for rain and\nthunderstorms continue, although the overall severe threat is\nfairly low. Global ensembles favor the southeastern half of the\narea with highest chance (>50%) for over 1.00\" of rain through\nthe week. Localized amounts of 2-3\" are possible, although it is\npossible that the heaviest rain totals are realized to our east.",
